WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:12.260
- I am amazingly grateful

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:15.910
for this moment

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:22.660
to be introduced to my great friend.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.390
The most incredible, brilliant actress

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:29.901
that I've had an opportunity
to work with, is special,

00:00:29.901 --> 00:00:31.651
(audience clapping)
Alfre Woodard.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:37.650
As Sam Jackson

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.793
stood and talked about his work,

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:47.010
I remember Sam Jackson and myself

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:49.750
were in a play reading

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:52.323
at the Frank Sivero Workshop in New York.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.800
And who was directing that play reading?

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.983
Morgan Freeman, 1980.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:06.550
One of the incredible things

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:08.190
about receiving such an award

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:11.453
is what to say.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:16.593
The many speeches that I had planned,

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:23.543
this whole presentation was
my speech I think in some way.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.033
But outside of that,

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:31.920
I respect and I'm honored by the Academy

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.753
to present me with the
Jean Hersholt Award.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.563
And, at this particular moment in time,

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:45.283
we can think about our past,

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:49.290
we can remember our past.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:53.010
But one of the things
that I've come through

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.760
through my wonderful grandparents

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:59.900
who didn't allow their daughters and son

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:02.560
to pick cotton in September,

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.993
and sent them to school in September.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.840
And because of that,

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:13.840
these heroic, amazing people
in Louisville, Georgia,

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:18.340
she was, in fact, only the first person

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:22.873
to graduate from college in her
family and in her community.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:28.710
And she inspired me,

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:33.400
my mother, because no one worked harder,

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:34.983
no one dreamed harder,

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:40.140
and no one just fought for justice.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.610
My mother was the regional president

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:46.593
of the National Council of
Negro Women in San Francisco.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.220
Her chapter raised more
money than any chapter

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:54.200
in the country for the
Mary McLeod Bethune statue,

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:55.440
which stands outside of

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:04.060
the organization's building right there.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:09.060
My dad, the most incredibly
beautiful man that I ever met,

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:13.493
falling in love with him
when I was five years old.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:17.100
An endless dream that was never separated

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.603
in that period of time till
he took his last breath.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:27.990
I listened to the amazing

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:30.983
Liv Ullmann.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.040
(audience clapping)

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:42.653
Just the feeling to be on,

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:46.557
at this stage, and to stand with her,

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.180
it's just amazing.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:56.980
It's just one of the
great memories of my life.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:58.320
Like her movies,

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:02.560
were one of the great
memories of my life as well.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:04.320
When you wanted to think about

00:04:05.562 --> 00:04:09.400
what are the things you wanna
do in front of the camera?

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.380
Having no idea that I would ever be

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.333
in front of the camera at that time.

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:19.210
And what are some of the
feelings, universal feelings,

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:23.410
that she's displayed that
went beyond just Sweden,

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:26.750
but carried us through our development

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:29.513
in this part of human development as well?

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:32.513
Those are things that happened.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:38.740
The opportunity to work with Alfre Woodard

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.630
on "Mandela" and other
cases in other places.

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:46.980
The opportunity to work with her

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:50.583
has been one of the great
pleasures in my career.

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:54.230
To have so much

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:01.760
incredible energy, and incredible wisdom,

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.810
and an incredible love for what she does,

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:08.213
and for humanity, has been incredible.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:12.950
I've also had a rich relationship

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:16.640
with organized labor.

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:17.823
As I said before,

00:05:19.559 --> 00:05:21.476
if it's mentioned here,

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:26.420
my mother and father,
James and Carrie Glover,

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:28.800
came at a point in time

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:31.960
when the labor movement in San Francisco

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:36.620
gave them a sense of purpose,

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:40.210
raising a family and having the sense

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:42.800
that they were in that bridge,

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:47.103
they were connected to a larger
story, a larger narrative.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:52.463
I have here with me, a
great friend of mine,

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:54.633
George Gresham,

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:59.760
who is the president
and executive director

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.290
right here of SEIU 1199 in New York.

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:06.972
One of the great, great, great.

00:06:06.972 --> 00:06:08.990
(audience clapping)

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:12.870
And you would know that artists had a home

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:17.023
in 1199, historically,
with bread and roses.

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:21.180
I'm here, there in celebrating this,

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.490
'cause this belongs to them.

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:27.360
This belongs to the
workers that I had a chance

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:30.400
to sit with and to support

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:36.720
as they walked out on their
work for great justice

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:41.720
and a better working place in
the country where they were.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:47.460
I had the opportunity to
work with Bruce Raynor.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:51.327
Bruce Raynor, who brought me to Mexico,

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.530
where we sat on the floor

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:59.370
of a woman making $30 a week

00:07:02.699 --> 00:07:03.532
trying to feed her family,

00:07:04.499 --> 00:07:08.037
and have just a few of her seven kids

00:07:11.503 --> 00:07:12.336
go to school.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:16.680
Bruce Raynor has been an extraordinary

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:21.410
part of my life,

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:24.410
as he allowed me to sit in the back rooms

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:28.270
and sometimes to make the tough decisions

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:33.270
that have to be made to make
this a better workplace.

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:36.313
And then, also a better world as well.

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:40.310
Those are the ones whose
gifts that have been given me

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:44.930
in this particular moment, and in life,

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:47.800
and throughout that life.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:52.740
That strike of 1968, which began
as the Black Student Union,

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:55.990
which created the Third World Coalition

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:59.743
of African American,

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.030
Latino American,

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:07.040
Asian American, and white Americans

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:09.960
to talk about the truth aversion

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:14.960
of the 1619 Project of
critical race theory.

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:18.160
(audience clapping)

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.640
And how do you get here?

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:25.263
How do you get to this point?

00:08:30.737 --> 00:08:33.610
How do you move and keep moving?

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:37.170
It's because people move

00:08:42.017 --> 00:08:43.983
and they continue to move.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:50.180
I am proud of being a part of that.

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:52.610
And I'm proud of being someone

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:56.620
who was moved by the work of Athol Fugard

00:08:56.620 --> 00:08:59.733
in South Africa and his writings.

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:03.180
Which Zakes Mokae,

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:08.153
a wonderful South African actor,

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:13.270
Zakes Mokae took me around

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:15.410
to the homes and parties

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:20.810
that South Africans, exiled, would have

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:23.510
in San Francisco and the Bay Area.

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:26.913
And somewhere about midnight,

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:32.420
they began to have the music

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:36.890
of their homeland.

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:39.823
The speaking about homeland,

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:46.230
the tears and pain of being exiled here

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:48.690
not knowing what the future is

00:09:50.489 --> 00:09:52.089
at their homelands.

00:09:52.089 --> 00:09:55.427
And those stories moved us, moved me.

00:09:57.941 --> 00:10:02.440
And I was happy to be a part of that.

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:09.660
If I seem repetitive in my comments,

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:12.710
it's because I didn't know

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:14.509
that my speech was going to be here.

00:10:14.509 --> 00:10:17.030
(audience laughing)

00:10:17.030 --> 00:10:18.840
And so I haven't referred to this,

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:24.893
the teleprompter, at all,

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:31.033
because I don't.
(audience clapping)

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:36.023
And sometimes we, as actors,

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:39.438
become a little lost if
we don't have a script.

00:10:39.438 --> 00:10:41.750
(audience laughing)

00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:43.003
It brings us alive here.

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:53.370
As I've said,

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:57.730
with Samuel L. Jackson, Liv Ullmann,

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:04.530
and those who have graced our screens,

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:06.420
and those who continue,

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:11.343
I'm proud to be a part
of this space and place.

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:17.010
And thank you, thank you,

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:21.550
thank you, thank you.
(audience clapping)

